  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the file set covering both sides of the exception-provenance trace:\n\n**Netty pipeline exception propagation:**\n- `AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 the actual `exceptionCaught`/`fireExceptionCaught` propagation logic\n- `DefaultChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 pipeline construction and tail-context default exception handling (logs and drops if unhandled)\n- `ChannelPipeline.java`, `ChannelHandlerContext.java`, `ChannelInboundHandler.java`, `ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter.java`, `ChannelDuplexHandler.java` \u2014 the handler contract exceptions flow through\n\n**Spring WebFlux / reactor-netty bridge (spring-web module):**\n- `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 the actual adapter wrapping reactor-netty's `HttpServerRequest`/`HttpServerResponse` into Spring's reactive types; this is the seam where Netty-originated errors first enter Spring\n- `ReactorServerHttpRequest.java` / `ReactorServerHttpResponse.java` \u2014 request/response wrappers over the reactor-netty channel\n- `HttpHandler.java` \u2014 the top-level reactive contract (`Mono<Void> handle(...)`) that swallows/converts errors into a completion signal\n- `HttpWebHandlerAdapter.java` / `WebHttpHandlerBuilder.java` \u2014 where the `WebHandler` chain (including exception handling) is assembled for the server adapter\n- `AbstractServerHttpResponse.java` \u2014 where response commit/error state is tracked, relevant to where context (headers, cause) can get lost once the response is already committed\n- `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler.java` / `ResponseStatusExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 WebFlux's own exception-to-generic-500 translation layer, the likely site of context loss",
